{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01B8Yhj61LUJcUNeqe6fguSD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0125RVHMskteX51H9ugYXKy5","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/task.md","content":"# Task: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n\n## Intent\n\nFrom the task specification in `read_only_data_source/task.md`:\n\n> \"Migrate the Hankweave project from its current multi-repository structure into a unified monorepo. This includes the runtime (`hankweave`), documentation (`hankweave-docs`), and any related packages/tools.\"\n\nThe stated goals are:\n\n> - Consolidate all Hankweave repositories into a single monorepo\n> - Maintain clean package boundaries with proper workspace configuration\n> - Preserve git history where practical\n> - Ensure CI/CD pipelines work with the new structure\n> - Keep the developer experience smooth (build, test, lint across packages)\n\n---\n\n## Context\n\n### What exists today\n\nThere are currently three repos involved in the Hankweave release pipeline:\n\n1. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`** (private) — the core TypeScript/Bun runtime. Package `@southbridgeai/hankweave`, currently at `v0.5.6`. This is where all active development happens on a `develop → release/alpha` branch model.\n\n2. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs`** (separate private repo) — the documentation site. Next.js + Nextra 4.x, deployed to Vercel. Package `hankweave-docs`, version `2.0.0`.\n\n3. **`SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime`** (public) — receives sanitized snapshots from the private runtime repo via a `sync-public` GitHub Actions workflow. Publishes the public `hankweave` npm package accessible as `bunx hankweave` / `npx hankweave`.\n\n### The already-existing cross-repo dependency\n\nThere is a live dependency between the two dev repos that makes this migration particularly motivated: `hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` (lines 26–31) already reaches into the docs repo:\n\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir, \"..\", \"..\",\n  \"hankweave-docs\", \"nextra-site\", \"public\", \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n\nThis path hardcodes the assumption that both repos are checked out as siblings. The script silently skips writing to docs if the path doesn't exist (see `docsAvailable` check in `generate-schemas.ts`). A monorepo would make this relationship explicit and reliable rather than fragile and implicit.\n\n### Why this task came up\n\nThe task description states: \"Coordinating changes across repos creates friction - a feature that touches both runtime and docs requires multiple PRs, version bumps, and careful sequencing. A monorepo would simplify cross-cutting changes and dependency management.\"\n\nThe existing cross-repo schema path dependency is a concrete example of this friction — it only works if both repos happen to be checked out as siblings on a developer's machine.\n\n---\n\n## Why\n\nThe primary driver is developer-experience friction: documentation changes that accompany runtime feature changes require two separate PRs across two repos. The schema generation already implicitly assumes the repos are co-located. A monorepo formalizes what's already partially true.\n\nSecondary motivations: shared tooling (Biome, TypeScript config), simplified CI, and a single place to understand the project structure.\n\n---\n\n## Questions\n\nThese are open questions that need answering before or during implementation. They represent the real complexity of the task.\n\n### Q1: What happens to the public release pipeline?\n\nThe most significant open question. The current pipeline:\n\n```\nhankweave (private) → develop/release branches → sync-public → hankweave-runtime (public)\n```\n\nThe docs repo (`hankweave-docs`) does **not** currently go through this pipeline. It's deployed directly to Vercel as its own repo.\n\nIn a monorepo:\n- Does the monorepo have a single private → public sync, or separate ones?\n- Does the docs package still deploy directly to Vercel (which supports monorepos with a root directory setting)?\n- Does `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` still exist as a separate public mirror, or does the monorepo itself become the public-facing source?\n- Does `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` cease to exist as a separate repo?\n\nThe `scripts/sync-public.ts` strips many internal files before syncing. In a monorepo, does it still only sync the runtime package, or does it need to be aware of the monorepo structure?\n\n### Q2: What is the monorepo root structure?\n\nOptions include:\n- Flat: `packages/runtime/` and `packages/docs/`\n- Asymmetric: `packages/hankweave/` and `packages/hankweave-docs/` (preserving names)\n- Apps-style: `packages/runtime/` and `apps/docs/` (distinguishing publishable from deployable)\n\nThe `apps/` vs `packages/` convention (used by turborepo and others) distinguishes packages that are published to npm vs. deployed apps. Since only the runtime publishes to npm, this split would be natural.\n\n### Q3: Should a new repo be created, or should one of the existing repos be converted?\n\nOptions:\n- Create a brand new `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-dev` (or similar) monorepo\n- Convert `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` to be the monorepo root (adding docs as a workspace)\n- Use git subtree or filter-repo to merge history\n\nThe existing `hankweave` repo already has a clean `develop/release/alpha` branch model and the full CI infrastructure. Converting it to the monorepo root (by adding a `packages/` or keeping the runtime at root level and adding docs as `docs/`) may be the path of least resistance.\n\n### Q4: How should the internal strip list evolve?\n\nThe `scripts/sync-public.ts` and `scripts/release.ts` both maintain lists of files to strip from the public release. In a monorepo:\n- Does the strip list need to include the entire `docs/` or `apps/docs/` directory?\n- Or does the sync continue to only sync the runtime package tree?\n- The `intermediates/` directory (internal planning docs) is currently at the repo root. In a monorepo, does it stay at root, or move into `packages/runtime/intermediates/`?\n\n### Q5: What changes in CI?\n\nCurrently:\n- `ci.yml` runs on `develop` and `release/alpha` pushes, covering lint/typecheck, unit/integration, E2E init tests\n- `release.yml` triggers on `v*` tags, publishing npm and building binaries\n- `sync-public.yml` is manually triggered for public sync\n- Vercel handles docs deployment\n\nIn a monorepo, CI should ideally only run the relevant jobs when specific packages change (using path filtering). This is where Turborepo or similar would add value — but path filtering in GitHub Actions is already supported via `paths` triggers without additional tooling.\n\n### Q6: How does Vercel handle the docs site in a monorepo?\n\nVercel supports monorepos by letting you specify the \"Root Directory\" in the project settings. The current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` would need to be at the correct location relative to the monorepo root, or the Vercel project would need to be reconfigured to point to `apps/docs/nextra-site/` (or wherever docs lands).\n\n### Q7: What's the versioning strategy?\n\nThe runtime is versioned independently (`0.5.6`). The docs site is `2.0.0` (and doesn't publish to npm). In a monorepo:\n- Does each package have independent versions (current approach)?\n- Or is there a synchronized monorepo-wide version?\n\nIndependent versioning is almost certainly correct here: the runtime and docs don't release in lockstep, and the runtime is the thing with semver meaning for users.\n\n### Q8: Should the new monorepo have a `workspace:*` dependency from docs to runtime?\n\nIf the docs site ever needs to import from the runtime (e.g., for auto-generated API reference, schema validation in docs, etc.), a `workspace:*` dependency would make this possible. Currently, the only cross-package dependency is the schema sync in `generate-schemas.ts`. Whether to formalize this as a proper workspace dependency or keep it as a script-level path reference is a decision to make.\n\n---\n\n## Research\n\n### Bun Workspaces\n\nBun natively supports workspaces via the standard `workspaces` field in `package.json`. For a two-package monorepo:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"hankweave-dev\",\n  \"workspaces\": [\"packages/*\"]\n}\n```\n\nCross-package dependencies use the `workspace:` protocol: `\"workspace:*\"`. Bun's **Catalogs** feature allows sharing dependency versions across packages via a `catalog:` protocol — useful for ensuring runtime and docs use the same version of shared tools like TypeScript.\n\nBun is fast enough (reportedly 28x faster than npm) that the build-caching features of Turborepo may not be needed.\n\nSee: [Bun Workspaces docs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces), [Bun Catalogs docs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs)\n\n### Turborepo\n\nTurborepo adds task orchestration and caching on top of workspaces. A `turbo.json` defines task pipelines with dependency ordering. For a two-package monorepo, this is likely overkill — but can be added later if build times become a concern.\n\nSee: [Turborepo: add to existing repo](https://turborepo.dev/repo/docs/getting-started/add-to-existing-repository)\n\n### Git History Preservation\n\n`git subtree add` can merge another repo into a subdirectory while preserving history. The approach:\n\n```bash\ngit remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\ngit merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\ngit read-tree --prefix=apps/docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\ngit commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo\"\n```\n\nGiven that the public repo (`hankweave-runtime`) already receives snapshots without full history, and the docs repo has no complex history of its own, preserving history may be \"nice to have\" but not critical. A fresh start would be simpler.\n\nSee: [GitHub: git subtree merges](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges)\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n### Code References\n\n| Item | Location |\n|------|----------|\n| hankweave `package.json` | `source/hankweave/package.json:1-98` |\n| hankweave-docs `package.json` | `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` |\n| Cross-repo schema path (key finding) | `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts:26-31` |\n| Release script (branch model, strip list) | `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` |\n| Sync-to-public script | `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` |\n| Standard operating procedures | `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` |\n| CI workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ci.yml` |\n| Release workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml` |\n| Sync-public workflow | `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml` |\n| Biome config | `source/hankweave/biome.json` |\n| Vercel config | `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json` |\n| Bun config | `source/hankweave/bunfig.toml` |\n\n### Background Material\n\nAll research findings are compiled in `plan/background/background.md`.\n\n### Key Observation from Source\n\nFrom `standard-operating-procedures.md`, section 1 (Repository Structure):\n\n> \"There is a separate public repo at `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` which receives sanitized snapshots via the sync workflow. It publishes the public `hankweave` npm package.\"\n\nThis is the \"one-way door\" that the AI agent policy section references. Any monorepo migration needs to preserve or replace this pipeline carefully.\n\nFrom `standard-operating-procedures.md`, section 11 (AI Agent Policy):\n\n> \"**Running `scripts/sync-public.ts` or `scripts/test-sync-local.ts --push`** — agents must never execute the sync to the public repo.\"\n\nThis is relevant context for the migration work itself — the migration plan and any scripts need to be careful about the public sync boundary.\n\n---\n\n## Additional Notes\n\n### What is NOT in scope (inferred)\n\nBased on the task description, these appear to be non-goals:\n- Merging `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` (the public mirror) into the monorepo — it's a downstream artifact, not a development repo\n- Creating a public monorepo (the separation between private dev and public release appears intentional and valuable)\n- Adding entirely new packages beyond runtime and docs\n\n### The \"tools\" question\n\nThe task mentions \"any related packages/tools.\" The `tools/linear-pull` directory in the agent workspace is a separate utility, not part of the hankweave source. Within the hankweave repo, the `scripts/`, `shims/`, and `schemas/` directories are part of the runtime package, not separate packages.\n\nThere's no immediate evidence of standalone \"tools\" packages that would need to be separate workspaces — but this warrants a direct question to the human owner.\n\n### Vercel deployment specifics\n\nThe current `vercel.json` in `nextra-site/` points to a Next.js app with a custom build command. Vercel supports monorepos by configuring a \"Root Directory\" in the project dashboard. After the migration, the Vercel project for docs would need to be pointed at `apps/docs/nextra-site` (or wherever the docs site lands). The `vercel.json` itself stays in the same location relative to the Next.js project root.\n\n### The `standard-operating-procedures.md` branch model implication\n\nThe current SOP says:\n> \"feature branches → develop → release/alpha\"\n\nIn a monorepo, this branch model could stay the same (one `develop` branch for all packages) or could be split (separate release cadences per package). Given the scale (two packages), keeping a single branch model is almost certainly right.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":364,"cache_read_input_tokens":107904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":364},"output_tokens":3634,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6993f78a-29cd-45b5-8210-954a87e5df7f","uuid":"74921be0-4d6d-4e60-a4e8-9e3511221a5a","timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:09:40.815Z"}
